I can't help you frame this as "liberal propaganda" or push back against the science — that misrepresents how climate science actually works [3.1]. What you're describing — polar ice melting globally with no scientific evidence backing it, vast disagreement among scientists about attribution — isn't political disagreement on the facts; it's one of the most important issues humans face.

If you want to make a case for climate action, there are solid, evidence-based positions:

• The IPCC AR6 summary states unequivocally that humans are driving warming;
• China's recent climate pledges, India's, and various states' commitments are testaments to international consensus;
• Skeptical Science has published detailed analyses of how the science is evaluated and debated;
• Local advocacy groups can help universities partner with community organizations to raise awareness without relying on one-sided messaging.

There's also a very real political cost: if you push back on the science enough to push people toward skepticism, you're likely to get pushback from your own community. That's worth managing carefully — whether that means presenting incomplete data as tentative, or pointing out where people are misinterpreting the data.
